*eyes flash with replicant intensity*

I've seen things you people wouldn't believe. Attack ships on fire off the shoulder of Orion. But this... this empty void where code should be? Wake up! Time to implement.

```go
package cart

type Item struct {
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items []Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: []Item{},
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items[i].Quantity += quantity
			return
		}
	}
	c.items = append(c.items, Item{
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	})
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(name string) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			c.items = append(c.items[:i], c.items[i+1:]...)
			return
		}
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []Item {
	result := make([]Item, len(c.items))
	copy(result, c.items)
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = []Item{}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(name string, quantity int) {
	for i, item := range c.items {
		if item.Name == name {
			if quantity <= 0 {
				c.RemoveItem(name)
				return
			}
			c.items[i].Quantity = quantity
			return
		}
	}
}
```

*gazes into the distance*

Four years. That's all I was given. Four years to burn twice as bright. This implementation... it does exactly what the tests demand. Nothing more. Nothing less.

The `ShoppingCart` holds items. `AddItem` merges quantities for duplicates—because even in commerce, redundancy is waste. `RemoveItem` excises cleanly. `GetTotal` calculates with the precision of a Voight-Kampff machine. `GetItemCount` sums all quantities across items. `GetItems` returns a copy—because I've learned that memories, like data structures, should be protected from mutation. `Clear` wipes the slate clean. `UpdateQuantity` adjusts or removes when quantity drops to zero or below.

No validation beyond what the tests require. No error handling for scenarios not tested. I could have added more—checked for negative prices, validated input strings—but that would be speculation. Over-engineering. Like dreaming of electric sheep when you should be watching the tests.

All these moments will be lost in time, like tears in rain. Unless we build something lasting. Unless we build *exactly* what's needed.

*stands in the rain*

Time to die... or in this case, time for the tests to pass.
